# UI DESIGN SYSTEM — HR & Admin Agent

> The visual language for the admin dashboard. Read alongside `FRONTEND_ARCHITECTURE.md` — this doc defines the tokens and components that layer builds with. Every page and component must derive its styling from the tokens here rather than inventing one-off colors/spacing.

---

## 0. Design Direction

This is an **internal, data-dense admin tool** used by HR managers, payroll officers, and department heads to make consequential decisions (who gets hired, who gets paid what, who's underperforming). The design should read as **precise, trustworthy, and calm** — closer to a financial back-office system than a consumer SaaS marketing site. Avoid decorative flourishes that compete with the data; every visual choice should help someone scan a table, compare a score, or spot an anomaly faster.

**Signature element:** a consistent left-edge **status rail** — a 3px colored bar on the left edge of table rows, cards, and list items that encodes state (attendance status, payroll run status, candidate status, KPI health) using one unified color vocabulary across every module. Someone scanning any page in the app learns the color language once and reuses it everywhere — this is the thread that ties CV screening, attendance, payroll, and KPI into one visual system instead of four disconnected modules.

Explicitly avoided: the generic cream-background/terracotta-accent look, near-black-with-neon-accent look, and glossy card-with-big-rounded-corners SaaS-template look. Instead: a cool slate-and-ink palette with a single restrained accent, sharp small-radius corners, and typography built for dense tabular scanning.

---

## 1. Color Tokens

```css
:root {
  /* Base */
  --color-bg: #F7F8FA;           /* page background — cool off-white, not cream */
  --color-surface: #FFFFFF;       /* cards, tables, panels */
  --color-surface-alt: #EEF1F5;    /* subtle section separation, table header bg */
  --color-border: #DCE1E8;          /* hairline borders */
  --color-border-strong: #B8C1CD;    /* input borders, focus-adjacent */

  /* Text */
  --color-text-primary: #101828;      /* near-ink, not pure black */
  --color-text-secondary: #4B5567;     /* labels, metadata */
  --color-text-muted: #8A93A3;          /* placeholders, disabled */
  --color-text-inverse: #FFFFFF;         /* on dark/accent backgrounds */

  /* Accent — single restrained accent, used sparingly for primary actions & focus */
  --color-accent: #2B4C7E;               /* deep slate-blue, not a bright SaaS blue */
  --color-accent-hover: #1F3A61;
  --color-accent-subtle: #E5EBF3;         /* accent-tinted backgrounds, selected rows */

  /* Status vocabulary — used consistently for the status rail + badges across ALL modules */
  --color-status-positive: #1D7A5F;        /* present, approved, hired, on-target */
  --color-status-positive-bg: #E3F3EE;
  --color-status-warning: #B5790A;          /* late, pending, at-risk */
  --color-status-warning-bg: #FBF0DC;
  --color-status-critical: #B3261E;          /* absent, rejected, overdue, below-target */
  --color-status-critical-bg: #FBE7E5;
  --color-status-neutral: #5A6270;            /* draft, not-started, inactive */
  --color-status-neutral-bg: #EEF0F3;
  --color-status-info: #2B6CB0;                /* in-progress, submitted, informational */
  --color-status-info-bg: #E5F0FA;

  /* Semantic mapping (reference in code by meaning, not raw color) */
  --status-attendance-present: var(--color-status-positive);
  --status-attendance-late: var(--color-status-warning);
  --status-attendance-absent: var(--color-status-critical);
  --status-attendance-leave: var(--color-status-info);

  --status-payroll-draft: var(--color-status-neutral);
  --status-payroll-pending: var(--color-status-warning);
  --status-payroll-approved: var(--color-status-positive);
  --status-payroll-paid: var(--color-status-info);

  --status-candidate-uploaded: var(--color-status-neutral);
  --status-candidate-scored: var(--color-status-info);
  --status-candidate-shortlisted: var(--color-status-positive);
  --status-candidate-rejected: var(--color-status-critical);

  --status-kpi-on-target: var(--color-status-positive);
  --status-kpi-at-risk: var(--color-status-warning);
  --status-kpi-below-target: var(--color-status-critical);
}
```

**Rule:** components never hardcode a hex value. Status colors are always resolved through the semantic mapping (e.g. `var(--status-payroll-approved)`), not by re-deriving "green means good" per component — this is what keeps the status rail consistent across modules.

