## 5. Iconography

Single icon set throughout (recommend `lucide-react` for consistency with the rest of the stack — same family used in Visualizer/artifact contexts, MIT licensed, works cleanly with React). Icons are functional wayfinding (module nav, status indicators, table actions), never decorative illustration — this tool doesn't need hero illustrations or empty-state mascots; a plain, direct message is more appropriate for this audience (see writing guidance below).

---

## 6. Writing / Microcopy Conventions

- **Buttons name the action's result, not a generic verb:** "Approve Payroll Run," not "Submit." "Shortlist Candidate," not "Confirm."
- **Errors state what happened and what to do**, in the interface's voice: "This attendance record already exists for this date. Edit the existing record instead." — not "Error: conflict."
- **Empty states are instructive, not decorative:** "No candidates yet. Upload a CV to start screening for this role." with the relevant action button right there — not an illustration with vague copy.
- **Status labels are the same word everywhere they appear** — a payslip that is "Approved" is never called "Confirmed" or "Finalized" on another screen. One vocabulary, enforced by pulling status labels from a single shared constants file (`frontend/src/constants/statusLabels.ts`), not re-typed as strings per page.

---

## 7. Responsiveness & Accessibility Floor

- Sidebar collapses to icon rail below 1024px; tables become horizontally scrollable (not stacked cards — this audience needs to compare rows, stacking breaks that) below 768px.
- All interactive elements keyboard-reachable, visible focus ring (`--color-accent` outline) never suppressed.
- Color is never the only signal — status badges always carry text, KPI bars always carry a numeric value, not just a color fill.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` — transitions (hover states, modal open/close) are kept short (150–200ms) and simple regardless, no scroll-triggered animation anywhere in this tool.

---

## 8. What to Avoid

- No gradients, no glassmorphism, no large rounded corners on tables/cards.
- No stock illustration or mascot-style empty states.
- No more than one accent color — status colors are functional, not decorative, and the accent (`--color-accent`) is reserved for primary actions and focus states only.
- No animation for its own sake — every transition should communicate a state change, not add atmosphere. This is a tool people use for hours a day; visual quietness is a feature, not a lack of polish.
